WEBVTT

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.000
Before planning a migration,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.000
teams need to understand what cryptography is doing in each place.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.390
Key establishment is about helping two parties arrive at shared secret material over an

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:10.034
untrusted network.

00:00:10.034 --> 00:00:14.210
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM, is one way to support that role.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:14.845
Conceptually,

00:00:14.845 --> 00:00:18.620
one party creates protected key material that another party can recover with the right

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.235
private information,

00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:23.000
allowing both sides to derive a shared secret for later symmetric encryption.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Digital signatures play a different role.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.140
A signature lets a verifier check that data was signed by the holder of a private key and

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.000
that the signed data has not been modified.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
That supports authentication, integrity, software update trust, code signing,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
certificate authorities, secure boot chains, identity tokens, document signing,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.000
and device enrollment.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Signatures do not automatically provide confidentiality.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
They answer questions about origin and integrity, not secrecy.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
These roles appear in many systems that practitioners use every day.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
TLS uses key establishment and authentication to protect web sessions and APIs.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.250
VPNs and SSH use public-key mechanisms for remote access.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.000
Software updates and packages rely on signatures.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.918
Certificates bind keys to identities.

00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:05.000
Secure email, device authentication, identity platforms,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.050
and firmware signing all depend on cryptographic roles that may have to change at

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:09.975
different speeds.

00:01:09.975 --> 00:01:13.909
Replacing one algorithm is therefore rarely a simple find-and-replace operation.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:17.780
A KEM change can affect protocol negotiation, message size, performance,

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:18.954
and library support.

00:01:18.954 --> 00:01:22.000
A signature change can affect certificate formats, chain sizes,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.000
hardware security module behavior, storage, logging, and verification code.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.939
The lesson is practical: identify the role first, then choose the migration path.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:33.990
A team that treats all cryptography as one category will miss dependencies and create

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.000
avoidable surprises.
